---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.ts";
---

<Docs
  title="Getting started"
  description="Share a service, invite a guest, and what the guest sees."
>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lede">Three steps for you, one for your guest.</p>

  <h2>1. Open PeerShare and say who you are</h2>
  <p>
    Open <a href="https://peershare.httpeers.net">peershare.httpeers.net</a> in Chrome, Edge or
    Firefox, and type your name at the top. Guests see it on the invite and while they use your
    services. Sharing starts as soon as the page is open, and stops when you close the tab.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Share a service</h2>
  <p>
    Give it a <strong>name</strong> (what your guests see) and its <strong>address</strong>. The
    address decides how PeerShare reaches it:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Reached</th><th>Needs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>http://localhost:5173</code>, <code>http://192.168.1.20:3000</code>, <code>http://nas.local</code></td>
        <td>Through the <a href={u("/local-proxy")}>local proxy</a></td>
        <td>The proxy running on your computer</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>https://docs.example.org</code></td>
        <td>Directly, by the PeerShare page</td>
        <td>
          The site allows calls from <code>https://peershare.httpeers.net</code>, with the
          <code>x-peershare-guest</code> header (CORS)
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    PeerShare tests the service at once, the same way guests will reach it, and shows
    <strong>Working</strong>, <strong>Slow</strong> or <strong>Unreachable</strong>, with what to
    do about a problem. The first service is the <strong>default</strong>: guests see it first.
    You can pause a service with its switch; its menu has Edit, Test again, Make default and
    Remove.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      To try it with nothing installed, share the example site
      <code>https://peershare-demo.httpeers.net</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Invite a guest</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Invite</strong> makes a link and a QR code for one person. Send it any way you like;
    a chat message is fine. The link works <strong>once, for 24 hours</strong>. For the next
    person, make a new link. Who joined appears under <strong>Guests</strong>, where
    <strong>Revoke</strong> ends their access at once.
  </p>

  <h2>What your guest does</h2>
  <p>
    They open the link: it says who invited them, and they press <strong>Join</strong>. Their
    browser connects to yours, and your services open, one tab per service. Each service runs in
    its own isolated page, so it cannot see the guest's other tabs, or PeerShare itself.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Closing the tab keeps them in: reopening the link brings them back.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Close session</strong> leaves for good: their browser forgets the invite, and
      coming back needs a new one.
    </li>
    <li>If your tab is closed or sharing is off, they see that your computer isn't answering, and the page reconnects by itself when you are back.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stopping</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Stop sharing</strong> takes your services offline; your guests and their access are
    kept, and they reconnect when you start again. Closing the tab does the same. To end a
    guest's access for good, revoke them.
  </p>
</Docs>
